## Why This Is Asked
Assesses ability to architect privacy-preserving, low-latency ML systems at scale, including RAG, edge adaptation, and robust evaluation.

## Key Concepts
- Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG)
- DP-SGD and Secure Aggregation
- Federated Learning (FL) with edge adaptation
- Latency budgets and edge deployment
- Drift detection and rollout strategy

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo DP-FL update sketch
for (device of devices) {
  const g = computeGradients(device.localData, model);
  const gDP = applyDifferentialPrivacy(g, epsilon);
  sendToServer(gDP);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle user opt-out data policies in FL and on-device learning?
- How would you measure and mitigate concept drift across enterprise domains?

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses on-device streaming ASR design with privacy, domain adaptation, and real-time constraints. 

## Key Concepts
- Streaming encoder-decoder models, CTC decoding, lexicon and LM integration
- On-device quantization and energy profiling
- Federated updates with secure aggregation and privacy guarantees
- Drift detection via per-user and per-domain metrics

## Code Example
```javascript
function streamDecode(chunk, state) {
  const features = extractFeatures(chunk);
  const logits = model.encodeDecode(features, state);
  const text = decodeCTC(logits);
  return text;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate latency under varying network/CPU loads? 
- How would you roll out vocabulary updates without sacrificing privacy?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical baseline ML for streaming signals with delayed labels, focusing on CPU constraints and simple online learning.

## Key Concepts
- Online updates with partial_fit
- Drift detection (PSI/KS)
- Calibration (Platt scaling)
- Simple retraining cadence and data splits

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo: batch online update loop
function onlineUpdate(batch){
  model.partial_fit(batch.features, batch.labels);
  monitorDrift(batch.features);
  calibrateIfNeeded();
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adjust if latency budget tightens to 50 ms?
- How would you validate calibration over time?

## Why This Is Asked

Examines practical handling of delayed supervision, distribution drift, and safe deployment in production ML systems.

## Key Concepts

- Delayed feedback and label latency
- Concept drift and drift detectors (ADWIN-like)
- Online vs offline retraining
- Calibration and evaluation (AUROC, PR-AUC, FPR)
- Data/versioning and deployment safety

## Code Example

```javascript
// Implementation sketch
let model = initModel();
let buffer = [];
stream.on('event', async (e) => {
  const x = featuresFromEvent(e);
  const yHat = model.predict(x);
  storePrediction(e.id, yHat);
  buffer.push({id: e.id, x, timestamp: Date.now()});
});

// Drift detection
setInterval(() => {
  const recent = getRecentPredictions(window);
  const metrics = evaluateMetrics(recent);
  if (driftDetector.detect(metrics)) {
    triggerOfflineRetrain();
  }
}, 3600000);
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical lightweight baseline building for text routing under strict latency constraints, in a data-scarce regime with model calibration requirements. Also covers drift monitoring and retraining cadence.

## Key Concepts
- Lightweight text representation with a fixed 30-feature vector
- SGD-based multiclass logistic regression or tiny decision tree for efficiency
- Calibration via temperature scaling
- Drift detection using rolling holdout validation
- Deployment latency and monitoring

## Code Example
```javascript
// Training loop outline for SGD multiclass LR
const model = new SGDLogisticRegression({
  learningRate: 0.01,
  epochs: 100,
  batchSize: 32
});
model.fit(XTrain, yTrain);
// Temperature scaling for calibration
const temp = calibrateTemperature(model, XVal, yVal);
// Inference
const predict = (features) => {
  const start = performance.now();
  const probs = model.predictProb(features, temp);
  const latency = performance.now() - start;
  return { probs, latency };
};
```

## Why This Is Asked
Production anomaly systems face non-stationarity, sparse labels, and strict latency. This question tests end-to-end design, from data schema to deployment, plus practical techniques like self-supervised pretraining and online adaptation.

## Key Concepts
- Self-supervised pretraining for unlabeled streams
- Online adaptation with meta-learning
- Two-tower architecture for heterogeneous features
- Drift detection and safe rollout/rollback
- Latency budgeting and quantization

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example gating logic for latency-adaptive routing
function route(payload, limitMs) {
  const t0 = performance.now();
  const score = computeScore(payload);
  const dt = performance.now() - t0;
  if (dt > limitMs) return 'fallback';
  return score;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you evaluate drift detectors in an AB test?
- What failure modes could cause late anomaly alerts and how to mitigate?
